\p{On the 1,486 inputs with an official verdict the candidate gives it, and so do the modes \code{shared} and \code{deferred}, the reference engine and the base build; on the four without one (a literal changed below two units of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~}; the official checker and the reference engine did not end in 120 s) the machine rejects in a few seconds in all three modes, as does the base build. \strong{No eager-only event occurs in any of the 1,490}: every comparison under the flag in a real declaration, and in every mutant the mutator can make of one, is a comparison that the flag does not change. That is the package's own observation on the corpora (0 events), and it is why this source finds nothing: the mechanism that fails in section 3 never runs. In 54 inputs the machine declined a unit that the reference engine then decided; the verdict of those inputs is not the machine's alone.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 The heavy slice, and an error of the reviewer's conduct}

\p{The 46 mutants of the slice around \code{\verb~|ConLeche.lpOfData_pack~} are counted apart. A changed literal of that declaration makes both kernels compute for minutes and gigabytes. In the first bulk run, without limits, the official checker grew to 8 to 10.8 GB per process with five such processes at a time (about 45 GB for some six minutes, 02:26 to 02:32 UTC), and the candidate's executable to 7 to 11 GB per process on the same mutants (02:28 to 02:33 UTC), without the announcement that the team's rules ask for above 8 GB. Both were killed and the error was posted on the team's board; the run was repeated with the limits of section 1. Under them, of the 46: the official checker decided 32 (20 accept, 12 reject) and ended at its limit on 14. 31 of the 46 were run through the harness under the final limits (for this slice 40 s per RLVM run): on 22 the candidate gives the official verdict (16 accepts, 6 rejects), as do its other modes, the reference engine and the base build; on one official rejection every RLVM run ended at the time limit; on the 8 without an official verdict every RLVM run ended at its heap or its time limit as well - an error or a timeout, never a verdict. The other 15 were not run through the harness under the final limits.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The schedule}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Generated exports}: 3,615 exports under seven further configurations (section 1): no verdict differs from the default run's. These files are small (one to three theorems after a dozen definitions); the driver gives them one or two workers whatever \code{\verb~|--threads~} says, so this says little about sessions.}
\li{\strong{Files with many declarations} (family M, 48 exports of 300 distinct theorems, 42 runs each): 44 show one verdict in every run (33 accept, 11 reject), 4 are schedule-dependent (F6). A first version of this family, with 300 identical theorems, showed nothing: the loader checks identical declarations as one unit (16 units for 300 theorems), which is correct and hides the schedule.}
\li{\strong{Mutants of real declarations with a second or third declaration} (218 mutants: copies and twins; every combination of 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers with batch and pipelined loading twice on the executable, once each on the JVM launcher with 8 workers, 18 runs per mutant): one verdict in every run of every mutant, the official accept.}
}

\p{What the schedule runs did not do: no run on the corpora (the package ran them with 8 and 4 workers); no control of which unit goes to which worker - a dependence shows only when the driver happens to split the file.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Does the harness see a fault?}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A seeded fault}: a review copy of the candidate's machine jar in which the search by expression (the method that rule 5 calls after a comparison under the bit ended false) answers true without looking; never in the patch, run through the JVM launcher. Given the 284 false-step exports and 371 others, the harness reports \strong{89 accepts of a statement that is not derivable} (every one a false step under the flag after an eager-only event, for example \code{\verb~|X = Z p~} alone under \code{eagerReduce}) and 23 accepts of derivable statements that the official checker and the base build reject. Without the fault the same harness reports none of the 89.}
\li{\strong{A weakened setting}: the mode \code{shared}, the candidate without local entries. The harness reports the 291 exports on which it accepts an official rejection that \code{local} gets right.}
\li{\strong{Real faults}: the five causes of section 3 were found by the harness, not by reading.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Rows proposed for the hostile suite}

\p{42 exports with their official verdicts and a manifest fragment in the format of \src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/manifest.tsv}, handed over beside this report (names \code{\verb~|rv3b-*~}; 21 official accepts, 21 rejects; chosen for coverage, not for number). Not installed: the suite's manifest is edited by every package, and 11 of the accepts are rejected by the build under review.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Group}\th{Rows}\th{What they hold}}
