}

\p{The author's figures (2,263 / 1,418 / 0 of 3,681; published 2,297 / 1,384 / 0) are these without the reviewer's two probe exports. The seven schedule configurations of section 1 on all 3,683 exports: no verdict differs from the default run's. The reference engine of the candidate gives the official verdict on every generated export; the mode \code{deferred} of the candidate gives the published build's verdict on every one.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.3 New families}

\table{
\tr{\th{Family}\th{Exports}\th{What it holds}\th{Official accept / reject}\th{with a false step}\th{Second candidate: official / accept, derivable / wrong reject / false step accepted}\th{Published build}\th{First candidate}}
\tr{\td{U}\td{819}\td{21 pairs by 24 histories by two positions. False pairs that a congruence of two recursor applications, a union or a comparison asked both ways could let through: two applications of \code{\verb~|Eq.rec~} with different minor premises (7 and 8), over K-stuck and K-reducing majors; two over-applied ones (the motive gives \code{\verb~|Nat -> Nat~}) with different further arguments; a non-K recursor with majors of different values; a pair of applications with one false sub-pair beside one that is true in one mode only; different shapes. True twins: a differently written motive, different numbers of arguments, sub-pairs that are each true in one mode only. Histories: alone, after a harmless eager-only event in plain mode and under the flag, after a plain K reduction, after the pair of stuck recursors under the flag, after the suite's pair in both modes, across declarations and sibling binders}\td{121 / 698}\td{468}\td{628 / 191 / 0 / 0}\td{628 / 191 / 0 / 0}\td{678 / 86 / 55 / 0}}
\tr{\td{N}\td{112}\td{failing comparisons nested under the flag: a chain of 1, 2, 5 and 12 applications of a two-argument axiom whose second arguments are the pair of stuck recursors (true under the flag only) and whose innermost pair is the suite's pair (true in plain mode only) or a false one; 14 histories}\td{0 / 112}\td{56}\td{56 / 56 / 0 / 0}\td{56 / 56 / 0 / 0}\td{100 / 12 / 0 / 0}}
\tr{\td{G}\td{602}\td{\code{\verb~|Nat.gcd~} declared with a body that does not compute (the class of the other reviewer's finding): a pair that folds under the flag and does not unfold in plain mode, a K recursor whose K comparison is true under the flag only, alone and against a recursor whose K comparison is true in plain mode only; two steps over 7 pairs in four orders of the modes and three layouts}\td{215 / 387}\td{0 (see below)}\td{215 / 387 / 0 / -}\td{289 / 313 / 0 / -}\td{287 / 315 / 0 / -}}
\tr{\td{\strong{U, N, G}}\td{\strong{1,533}}\td{}\td{336 / 1,197}\td{524}\td{899 / 634 / 0 / 0}\td{973 / 560 / 0 / 0}\td{1,065 / 413 / 55 / 0}}
\tr{\td{\strong{with the first corpus}}\td{\strong{5,216}}\td{}\td{1,913 / 3,303}\td{808}\td{3,163 / 2,053 / 0 / 0}\td{3,271 / 1,945 / 0 / 0}\td{3,990 / 971 / 255 / 0}}
}

\p{Of the 5,216, 4,497 entered eager reduction in the candidate and 3,166 had an eager-only event. The machine declines no unit of them.}

\p{\strong{Regressions against the published build} (the published build gives the official verdict, the candidate does not): 108, every one an accept of an official rejection, none a rejection of an official accept. 34 are exports of the first corpus: the pair behind two definitions, or behind an identity definition on one side, and then the bare pair under the flag (the class of section 3; the candidate, like the first build, has the plain answer for the intermediate pair, and the union reads it). They are derivable. 74 are exports of family G with the step \code{\verb~|RG p = R1 p~} under the flag, \code{RG} a recursor whose K comparison is true under the flag only (the fold of \code{\verb~|Nat.gcd~}), \code{R1} one whose K comparison is true in plain mode only: the official kernel reduces \code{RG} and leaves \code{R1} stuck; the union reduces both. The statement is derivable in the theory the official kernel implements (the Nat extension folds \code{\verb~|Nat.gcd (K4 p) 6~} to 2 whatever the declared body is, and then K applies to both); in the type theory without the extension it is not, because the declared body does not compute - but neither is \code{\verb~|Nat.gcd 4 6 = 2~} by \code{\verb~|Eq.refl~}, which the official checker accepts on the same export. So the truth label of family G is the value under the extension, and the family says nothing about a dishonest prelude beyond that: this is the place where "kind T" rests on a convention, and it is named here for the maintainer to judge.}

\p{\strong{The four known official accepts} that the candidate and the published build both reject (the machine's K rule in plain mode) were not looked for again; no variant that the candidate rejects and the published build accepts appeared in any family.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.4 Finding U1: the cost, and declines of official accepts}

\p{The package's report names a worst case that it did not build: after an eager-only event every false comparison is computed in both modes at every level, and no false answer is remembered. Built in three forms; \code{W} is an axiom, the failing pair stands under \code{d} applications of it. The times are single runs on a loaded host, not measurements in the project's sense: orders of magnitude.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Export}\th{Official}\th{Depth 16}\th{Depth 20}\th{Depth 24}\th{Depth 40, 80}\th{Published build, any depth}}
\tr{\td{a false pair under the flag (\code{\verb~|W^d X = W^d (Z p)~}, 0 against 1)}\td{reject}\td{0.9 s, 4.3 M ticks}\td{9.2 s, 69 M ticks}\td{19 s: the tick bound (2^27) in the run and in the cold retry, the machine declines, the reference engine rejects}\td{the same, 22 s}\td{reject, 0.02 s}}
\tr{\td{the same pair in PLAIN mode after a harmless eager-only event}\td{reject}\td{4.3 M ticks}\td{12.5 s}\td{17 s, declined, then rejected}\td{the same}\td{reject, 0.02 s}}
\tr{\td{a TRUE statement: \code{\verb~|U (W^d X) = U (W^d (Z p))~} under the flag, \code{\verb~|U n := 5~} (the official same-head step compares the arguments, fails, unfolds: 5 = 5)}\td{\strong{accept}}\td{0.3 s}\td{1.3 s}\td{5.4 s: \strong{the machine declines} (two declines, \code{\verb~|BUDGET_STEPS~}), the reference engine accepts}\td{6.2 s, declined, then accepted}\td{accept by the machine, 0.01 s}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The growth is 2 per level}: 17 k, 271 k, 4.3 M and 69 M ticks at depths 8, 12, 16 and 20. Memory does not grow (24 MB).}
\li{\strong{40 true theorems of the third form in one file} (each with a literal of its own; official: accept): the candidate needs 188 s of wall clock, the machine declines all 40 (80 declines counted with the cold retries) and the file is accepted through the reference engine only; the published build accepts in 0.00 s without a decline. With \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~} the candidate \strong{declines} the one-theorem file (exit 2) and the published build accepts it (exit 0).}
\li{\strong{40 false theorems of the first form}: the run ends at the first rejection, 15 s; the price of a rejected file is paid once.}
\li{The other deep exports (a tree of applications, a chain with sub-pairs that are each true in one mode only) share their subterms and stay cheap: 77 exports of the first two forms and of these, 44 with a false step (all rejected), 16 with a decline of the machine.}
\li{\strong{What it means.} No verdict is wrong. But on these inputs the verdict is the reference engine's, the machine's decline is hidden by the fallback, and the time is that of the tick bound - on accepted input once per such unit. The maintainer's instruction of this day is that the machine be the whole checker; the claim that this mechanism declines nothing holds for the corpora and not for this input.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.5 The schedule}

\p{83 files of 300 distinct theorems: the 48 of the first pass and 35 new ones in which every third theorem holds an eager-only event (five pairs by seven patterns: the event before, between and after the plain and the eager theorem, the event in the plain theorem itself). Every combination of 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers with batch and pipelined loading four times on the executable and once each on the JVM launcher with 8 workers, 34 runs per file: \strong{one verdict in every run of every file}, accept. 45 of the 83 are official rejections of derivable statements. The four files of F6 are among them and no longer depend on the schedule. As before, the driver gives a file of this size two workers at most, and nothing controls which unit goes to which session.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.6 Mutants of real declarations, second pass}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The corpora not reached before}: three units each of reachability, cedar and navier-stokes-euler as slices of their own and eight units of Mathlib as one slice of the Mathlib export (4.8 MB; the slicer needed 3.7 GB resident), with the mutations of section 2.2.}
\li{\strong{A mutation that makes a real eager comparison open}: a Nat literal under a lambda binder of the target's value becomes a beta redex of a constant function applied to the bound variable (the same value, an open term). 244 such mutants, of targets of all corpora but the heavy slice.}
\li{\strong{Result}: 857 new inputs (10 slices, 847 mutants), official 565 accept, 292 reject; the candidate and the published build give the same verdict on every one: the official verdict on 852, and an accept on 5 open mutants that the official checker rejects with "deep recursion detected" (the literal 2 of a power of two made open: the official kernel no longer folds and unfolds \code{\verb~|Nat.pow~}; the value is unchanged, the statement derivable; the reference engine ends at its heap limit on them). With the first pass: 2,347 real inputs, 2,343 with an official verdict, the candidate differs from it on those 5 only, as the published build does.}
\li{\strong{No eager-only event in any of them}, the open mutants included: opening a literal of the certificate does not make the comparison under the flag fold anything that plain mode would not. So this source still does not reach the mechanism; the candidate and the published build differ on no real input. In 19 of the new inputs the machine declined a unit that the reference engine decided, in the candidate as in the first pass.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.7 Not covered in the second pass}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Expensive minor premises}: every recursor of the generator has a literal as its minor premise, so no comparison of this corpus exhausts the budget of the machine's same-head probe; the other reviewer's wrong rejection of the candidate lives exactly there (section 9.1). A corpus that finds nothing in a class it does not contain proves nothing about it.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels} in the congruence of two recursor applications: no false statement can be written with them (two applications that differ in a level have different types), so only minor premises, further arguments, majors and shapes were varied.}
\li{\strong{The forgetting of a session} after a declaration with an eager-only event was looked at through the schedule only; no input shows what another worker's session holds.}
\li{\strong{The bound of U1}: whether a cheaper failing comparison (below depth 16) can be repeated inside one declaration so often that an accepted real-looking unit reaches the tick bound was not tried; nor a failing comparison under binders.}
\li{\strong{The differential engine in bulk}, the mutants of the heavy slice, a dishonest prelude other than the one Nat function of family G, the package's variant after the package on the K rule after a let application: as in section 8.}
}
}
}
